You can build and attack a complete multi-node ad hoc mesh network on a single Ubuntu machine without buying extra radios. By combining the Linux kernel's virtual Wi-Fi simulator (mac80211_hwsim), Network Namespaces, and B.A.T.M.A.N. Advanced (batman-adv), you can isolate multiple virtual wireless nodes, run real routing daemons, capture raw 802.11/mesh frames, and test exploits locally.

1. Lab Architecture on Ubuntu

To simulate distant devices on one host, you stack three Linux networking features:
• mac80211_hwsim: A kernel module that creates virtual Wi-Fi radios (wlan0, wlan1, wlan2) capable of real 802.11 IBSS (ad hoc) and 802.11s mesh frames.
• Network Namespaces (ip netns): Isolates each virtual radio into its own independent network stack so they cannot cheat by talking over lo.
• wmediumd (Optional): A user-space medium simulator that lets you drop packets or adjust signal strength between virtual nodes to simulate distance and hidden-node topologies.

2. Setting Up a 3-Node Virtual Ad Hoc Mesh

Run these commands on your Ubuntu machine to spin up three isolated wireless nodes (node1, node2, node3) running IBSS and batman-adv:
bash
# 1. Install required mesh and wireless tools
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y batctl iw iproute2 tcpdump wireshark

# 2. Load the virtual Wi-Fi driver with 3 radios and the batman-adv kernel module
sudo modprobe mac80211_hwsim radios=3
sudo modprobe batman-adv

# 3. Create 3 isolated network namespaces
for i in 1 2 3; do sudo ip netns add node$i; done

# 4. Move each virtual PHY radio into its own namespace
# (Check 'iw dev' first to verify phy0, phy1, phy2 map to wlan0, wlan1, wlan2)
sudo iw phy phy0 set netns name node1
sudo iw phy phy1 set netns name node2
sudo iw phy phy2 set netns name node3

# 5. Configure IBSS (Ad Hoc) + batman-adv inside each namespace
for i in 1 2 3; do
    WLAN="wlan$((i-1))"
    sudo ip netns exec node$i ip link set lo up
    sudo ip netns exec node$i ip link set $WLAN down
    sudo ip netns exec node$i iw dev $WLAN set type ibss
    sudo ip netns exec node$i ip link set $WLAN up
    sudo ip netns exec node$i iw dev $WLAN ibss join adhoc-lab 2412
    
    # Attach the ad hoc radio to a bat0 mesh interface
    sudo ip netns exec node$i batctl if add $WLAN
    sudo ip netns exec node$i ip link set bat0 up
    sudo ip netns exec node$i ip addr add 10.10.10.$i/24 dev bat0
done
Use code with caution.
To verify your mesh routing table and confirm the nodes see each other over virtual RF:
bash
sudo ip netns exec node1 batctl o   # View OGM originator table
sudo ip netns exec node1 ping -c 3 10.10.10.3
Use code with caution.

3. Offensive Security & Vulnerability Research Targets

Once the lab is running, you can attach Wireshark, Scapy, or custom C fuzzers directly to hwsim0 (the global monitor interface created automatically by mac80211_hwsim) or inside a rogue namespace (node3).
• Routing Protocol Fuzzing (batman-adv / OLSRd / Babel): Ad hoc protocols parse untrusted Layer-2 or UDP control frames (such as batman-adv Originator Messages/OGMs or olsrd HELLO/TC packets) directly in kernel space or privileged daemons. Inject malformed TLV headers, oversized packet lengths, or invalid sequence numbers from node3 using Scapy to hunt for kernel panics, out-of-bounds reads, or memory corruption.
• Blackhole & Sinkhole Routing Attacks: Configure node3 to advertise fake sequence numbers or artificially low metric costs so node1 and node2 route all traffic through your attacker node for interception.
• Wormhole & Replay Attacks: Capture valid routing advertisements from node1 on hwsim0 and replay them inside node3 to poison neighbor tables and cause routing loops or denial of service.
• 802.11 IBSS / 802.11s Frame Parsing Bugs: Because mac80211_hwsim passes frames through the real Linux mac80211 wireless stack, fuzzing management frames (beacons, probe responses, peering frames) tests the host kernel's wireless parser directly. (Note: Run this inside an Ubuntu VM if you are actively fuzzing kernel modules so a kernel panic does not crash your primary workstation.)

4. Cleanup Command

When you finish testing, tear down the lab cleanly with:
bash
for i in 1 2 3; do sudo ip netns del node$i; done
sudo modprobe -r batman-adv mac80211_hwsim
Use code with caution.
